lesson: 172
title: Working backwards through three steps
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: To find the start, begin with what is left at the end and undo the steps one by one, from the last to the first.
explain: Undo «took away 5» by adding 5, undo «halved» by doubling, undo «added 3» by taking 3 away, undo «doubled» by halving.
explain: She spent 4 dollars, then half of the rest, then 3 more, and had 6 left: 6 + 3 = 9, 9 × 2 = 18, 18 + 4 = 22 dollars at first.
vars: a=name l=1..20 k=2..12 j=2..12
let: s = 2 * (l + j) + k
answer: s
exercise: {a} spent {k} dollars on a ticket, then half of the money that was left on lunch, and then {j} dollars on a book. {a.He} has {l} dollars left. How many dollars did {a.he} have at first?
exercise: At the first stop {k} people got off a bus. At the second stop half of the people still on the bus got off, and at the third stop {j} more got off. Now {l} people are on the bus. How many people were on the bus at the start?
exercise: On Monday {a} read {k} pages of a book, on Tuesday half of the pages that were left, and on Wednesday {j} pages. {l} pages are still unread. How many pages does the book have?
exercise: I think of a number. I take away {k}, divide by 2 and then take away {j}. I get {l}. What was my number?
exercise: I think of a number, add {k} to it, double the result and add {j}. I get {2*(s+k)+j}. What number did I think of?
exercise: {a} gave {k} marbles to a friend, then half of the marbles that were left to {a.his} sister, and then lost {j} marbles. {a.He} has {l} marbles now. How many marbles did {a.he} have at first?
test: Guests ate {k} cookies from a jar, the children took half of what remained, and Dad ate {j} more. {l} cookies are left in the jar. How many cookies were in the jar to begin with?
test: Some number, made {k} smaller, then halved, then made {j} smaller, becomes {l}. Which number was it?
test: After three days of selling, a farmer has {l} sacks of potatoes left. On the third day he sold {j} sacks, on the second day half of the sacks he had that morning, and on the first day {k} sacks. How many sacks did he have before the first day?
test: {a} added {k} to a secret number, doubled the sum and then added {j} more, getting {2*(s+k)+j}. What was the secret number?
exercise: {a} had some money. {a.He} spent {k} dollars at a fair in Ohio, then half of what remained on a gift, then {j} dollars on snacks, and went home with {l} left. How many dollars did {a.he} start with?
exercise: A bus in London had some passengers. {k} got off at the first stop, half of the rest got off at the second stop, and {j} got off at the third, leaving {l} on board. How many were on board at first?
exercise: How many pages are in a book if {a} read {k} pages on the first day, half of the remaining pages on the second day, {j} pages on the third day, and still has {l} to read?
exercise: Take away {k} from a number, halve the result, then take away {j}, and you get {l}. What is the number?
exercise: A number is increased by {k}, the sum is doubled, and then {j} is added. The answer is {2*(s+k)+j}. What was the number?
exercise: {a} handed out {k} stickers to classmates in Toronto, then gave half of the stickers left to {a.his} brother, and then gave away {j} more, ending up with {l}. How many did {a.he} have in the beginning?
exercise: A farmer in New Zealand sold {k} lambs, then half of the lambs that remained, and then {j} more lambs, and has {l} left. How many lambs did he have to start with?
exercise: {l} is what you get when you subtract {k} from a number, divide by two and subtract {j}. Find the number.
test: At a bake sale in Dublin, {k} muffins were sold in the first hour, half of the remaining muffins in the second hour and {j} in the third hour, and {l} were left unsold. How many muffins were there at the start?
test: What number did {a} start with if {a.he} subtracted {k}, halved the result, subtracted {j} and ended with {l}?
test: {a} had a box of crayons. {a.He} lent {k} to a friend, gave half of the rest to {a.his} cousin, and lost {j}. Now {a.he} has {l}. How many crayons were in the box at first?
test: In a library in Sydney, {k} books were borrowed in the morning, half of the books left on the shelf were borrowed at noon, and {j} more in the evening, and the shelf still holds {l}. How many books were on the shelf before any were borrowed?
test: A number plus {k}, multiplied by 2, plus {j}, gives {2*(s+k)+j}. What is the number?
test: A jug held some cups of juice. {a} poured out {k} cups, then half of the cups that were left, then {j} cups more, and the jug still holds {l}. How many cups of juice were in the jug at first?
exercise: spent {k} on a ticket, then half what's left on lunch, then {j} on a book, got {l} left. how much i had
exercise: bus: {k} got off 1st stop, half the rest off at 2nd, {j+1} — no wait, {j} at 3rd, {l} left on. how many at start
exercise: Sam says just add em, {k}+{j}+{l}={k+j+l}. nah wrong innit, forgot the half. read {k} pages mon, half what's left tue, {j} wed, {l} still to go. pages in the book?
exercise: spent {k} dollar on a ticket (concert was 3 hours lol), half the rest on lunch, {j} on a book, {l} left. start money?
test: {l} people left on the bus after: {k} off at stop 1, half the rest at stop 2, {j} at stop 3. how many to begin
test: {a} ate {k} sweets, gave half the rest to {a.his} sis, then {j} more gone, {l} left. how many {a.he} had
test: read {k} pages, then half the rest, then {j}, and there's {l+3} — no, {l} pages left unread. how long's the book
exercise: Grandma passed away and left {a} some money. {a} spent {k} dollars on flowers for her grave, then half of what was left on a frame for her photo, and then {j} dollars on a book she loved. Now {a.he} has {l} dollars. How many dollars did Grandma leave {a.him}?
exercise: after dad got let go we had a bit of cash. paid {k} on the gas bill, half of the rest on rent, then {j} on bread n milk, now {l} left. how much did we start with, bloody hell
test: {a} was economical with the truth and said {a.he} spent "hardly anything". In fact {a.he} paid {k} dollars for a ticket first, then gave half of the remaining money for sweets, and last paid {j} dollars for a game, keeping {l} dollars. How many dollars did {a.he} begin with?
exam: It is given that {a} expended {k} dollars upon a ticket, subsequently one half of the remaining sum upon lunch, and thereafter {j} dollars upon a book, the balance remaining in dollars being {l}. Determine the sum in the possession of {a} at the outset, in dollars.
exam: Whereas {k} passengers alighted from a bus at the first stop, one half of those then remaining alighted at the second, and {j} further passengers at the third, the number remaining aboard being {l}, ascertain the number of passengers aboard at the outset.
exam: Ascertain the number from which, upon the subtraction of {k}, the halving of the result, and the subsequent subtraction of {j}, the number {l} is obtained.
exam: A baker sold {k} loaves in the first hour, one half of the remaining loaves in the second hour and {j} loaves in the third hour, whereupon the number of loaves remaining unsold was {l}. State the number of loaves with which the baker commenced the day.
